from common import Batch
b=Batch('REACT',51)
def q(*args,**kw): b.add(*args,**kw)
q('M','Refs','React 19 ref prop','In React 19, how can a function component receive a ref from its parent?','Accept ref as a prop and pass it to the intended node or use it in useImperativeHandle.','React 19 supports ref as a prop for function components. A new component does not need forwardRef solely to receive it.','Use the current function-component API.','Which prop now carries the reference?','react:reference/react/forwardRef',wrong=['Convert the component to a class.','Read the ref from props.key.','Access a global React.currentRef variable.'])
q('W','Refs','Imperative handle','Write FocusInput accepting ref and exposing only focus(), while keeping its DOM ref internal.','import { useImperativeHandle, useRef } from "react";\n\nexport default function FocusInput({ ref }) {\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({\n    focus() { inputRef.current?.focus(); }\n  }), []);\n  return <input ref={inputRef} aria-label="Search" />;\n}','Keep the DOM node in a private ref.~Expose a narrow handle through useImperativeHandle.~The parent can focus without receiving every DOM operation. Prefer declarative props when they express the interaction.','Separate the public handle from the DOM node.','Which Hook customizes the ref value?','react:reference/react/useImperativeHandle','// React 19 function component.\n// Implement FocusInput({ ref }).')
q('B','IDs','useId for list keys','Why is generating list keys this way invalid, and what should replace the expression?','useId cannot be called in this loop and is not a data-key generator. Use: <Row key={item.id} item={item} />;','The loop can change Hook call count.~useId is intended for accessibility relationships, not record identity.~Stable IDs from the data preserve list identity.','Separate accessibility IDs from data IDs.','Can list length change Hook order?','react:reference/react/useId#caveats','return items.map(item => (\n  <Row key={useId()} item={item} />\n));')
q('C','IDs','Hydration-safe IDs','Why use useId for label and input relationships instead of incrementing a module-level counter during render?','useId coordinates IDs across server rendering and hydration. A mutable counter depends on render order and replay. The server and client component trees still need to match.','A random or order-dependent ID can differ between server and client. useId solves identity generation for relationships, not mismatched application structure.','Server and client must agree.','Could rendering occur in a different order?','react:reference/react/useId#using-the-same-id-prefix-on-the-client-and-the-server')
q('S','State','Nested component definitions','A text field loses its draft whenever its parent rerenders. The child component function is declared inside that parent. Why?','Each parent render creates a new component function identity. React treats it as a different component type at that position. Move the child definition to module scope and pass inputs as props.','Stable JSX position is not enough when the component type changes. Memoizing unrelated data does not fix the type identity problem.','Component type participates in identity.','Is the function object stable between renders?','react:learn/preserving-and-resetting-state#different-components-at-the-same-position-reset-state')
q('T','Reducers','Action sequence','Run these pure reducer calls. What is logged?','5','The first add produces count 2.~reset replaces it with zero.~The final add produces 5. Dispatch order matters; actions are not merged as object patches.','Apply actions in order.','Does reset add or replace?','react:learn/extracting-state-logic-into-a-reducer','function reducer(count, action) {\n  return action.type === "reset" ? 0 : count + action.amount;\n}\nlet count = 0;\nfor (const action of [\n  { type: "add", amount: 2 },\n  { type: "reset" },\n  { type: "add", amount: 5 }\n]) {\n  count = reducer(count, action);\n}\nconsole.log(count);')
q('C','Events','Event-time side effects','Why should submitting an order usually happen in the submit handler rather than an Effect watching a submitted flag?','The submission is caused by a specific interaction. The handler has the event’s relevant data and intent. An Effect introduces synchronization that can replay or become detached from that intent.','Effects are appropriate when keeping an external system synchronized with rendering. They are not a default replacement for event handlers.','Identify the cause of the work.','Is it an interaction or ongoing synchronization?','react:learn/separating-events-from-effects')
q('M','Portals','Portal event ancestry','A portal renders outside its parent’s DOM subtree. Where do React events propagate?','Through the React component tree.','Portals change physical DOM placement without changing React ancestry. Context and React event propagation follow the React tree.','There are two trees.','Which tree owns the React relationship?','react:reference/react-dom/createPortal',wrong=['Only through the portal’s physical DOM ancestors, with no React ancestry.','They never propagate from a portal.','They always propagate to every React root.'])
q('S','Forms','State granularity','Typing in a search field rerenders a large unrelated dashboard. What structural change should be considered before memoizing every child?','Move the input state closer to the input where possible. Keep unrelated expensive content outside that frequently updated subtree. Lift only the state that other consumers actually need.','State placement controls which component begins the update. Broad memoization can add complexity without addressing unnecessarily high ownership.','Inspect the state owner.','Which subtree truly needs every keystroke?','react:reference/react-dom/components/input#optimizing-re-rendering-on-every-keystroke')
q('B','Memoization','Comparator stale callbacks','Why can this custom comparator leave the child calling an outdated handler?','It ignores onSelect. Compare every prop, including functions, or remove the custom comparator.','Returning true permits reuse of the prior render.~The old onSelect can retain old parent state.~A comparator must preserve equivalent behavior, not just equivalent labels.','Function props carry closures.','Can equal text hide a changed callback?','react:reference/react/memo#specifying-a-custom-comparison-function','const Row = memo(RowView, (previous, next) =>\n  previous.label === next.label\n);')
q('C','State','Batching boundary','Does automatic batching mean React merges every user interaction into one update?','No, batching groups updates within supported scheduling contexts. Separate intentional user events are handled separately. React preserves event-level behavior such as disabling a button before a later click.','Batching reduces intermediate renders but does not erase interaction boundaries. Do not depend on an exact render count as a correctness condition.','Distinct events carry distinct intent.','Could the next click need the committed result?','react:learn/queueing-a-series-of-state-updates#react-batches-state-updates')
q('S','External stores','Subscription Hook','Why use useSyncExternalStore instead of a hand-written Effect to read a mutable external store during concurrent rendering?','It provides a defined snapshot-and-subscription contract. React can detect changes around rendering and keep the view consistent. Supply a server snapshot as well when server rendering requires it.','An Effect subscribes after rendering and can miss consistency requirements. The snapshot must remain stable while the store has not changed.','A store can change outside React.','How does React validate the snapshot it rendered?','react:reference/react/useSyncExternalStore')
q('B','External stores','Uncached snapshot','What is wrong with returning a fresh object from every getSnapshot call?','It reports a new snapshot even when the store is unchanged. Cache the snapshot and return the same object until relevant data changes.','React compares snapshots by Object.is.~A fresh object always appears changed.~That can cause repeated renders; mutating one cached object without changing identity is also incorrect.','Snapshot identity is a change signal.','Does unchanged data return an unchanged identity?','react:reference/react/useSyncExternalStore#im-getting-an-error-the-result-of-getsnapshot-should-be-cached','const snapshot = useSyncExternalStore(\n  store.subscribe,\n  () => ({ count: store.count })\n);')
q('C','Errors','Error boundary scope','What failures should a React error boundary be expected to catch?','It catches rendering errors in its descendant component tree. It shows a fallback instead of the failed subtree. It does not generally catch event-handler errors or arbitrary asynchronous callback failures.','A boundary does not replace try/catch in event-driven async workflows. It also cannot recover its own failing fallback by catching itself.','Locate the failure phase.','Did it happen while rendering descendants?','react:reference/react/Component#catching-rendering-errors-with-an-error-boundary')
q('S','Errors','Boundary placement','Where should boundaries be placed on a dashboard with independent panels?','Place them around useful recovery regions. A failed panel can then show a local fallback while other panels remain usable. Keep a broader boundary for failures outside those regions.','One boundary around every tiny element creates noisy recovery behavior. One app-wide boundary can unnecessarily remove healthy parts of the interface.','Choose a meaningful failure region.','What can remain useful after one panel fails?','react:reference/react/Component#catching-rendering-errors-with-an-error-boundary')
q('M','Lazy loading','lazy module contract','What must a promise returned by a React lazy loader resolve to?','An object whose default property is a valid component type.','React lazy uses the resolved default export as the component. A named export can be adapted explicitly, but returning an arbitrary JSX element is not the module contract.','Think about a module namespace.','Which export does lazy consume?','react:reference/react/lazy',wrong=['A rendered DOM node.','Any string containing HTML.','A callback that directly mutates the root element.'])
q('B','Lazy loading','lazy declaration location','Why can this lazy component reset state, and where should its declaration move?','Creating the lazy type during render changes its identity. Move const Editor = lazy(...) to module scope.','Each parent render creates a new lazy component object.~React may treat it as a different type and reset state.~A stable module-level declaration also avoids redefining the loader wrapper.','Component type must remain stable.','Where can the lazy declaration run once?','react:reference/react/lazy#my-lazy-components-state-gets-reset-unexpectedly','function Workspace() {\n  const Editor = lazy(() => import("./Editor.js"));\n  return <Editor />;\n}')
q('W','Lazy loading','Suspense code split','Write a complete module that lazily imports a default-exported Reports component and shows Loading reports while it loads.','import { lazy, Suspense } from "react";\n\nconst Reports = lazy(() => import("./Reports.js"));\n\nexport default function ReportsPage() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading reports</p>}>\n      <Reports />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}','The lazy loader begins when React first tries to render Reports.~Suspense displays the fallback while the import is pending.~A rejected import needs an error boundary; Suspense handles waiting, not the final error fallback.','The loading boundary surrounds the lazy child.','Keep the component type outside render.','react:reference/react/lazy#suspense-for-code-splitting','// ./Reports.js exists and exports a component as default.\n// Implement ReportsPage.')
q('U','Effects','Effects on the server','Follow-up to REACT-020: can a server render rely on an Effect to fetch content before generating its initial HTML?','No, Effects do not run during server rendering. Use the framework’s server data-loading mechanism for initial server content. Client Effects can synchronize after the client commits.','An Effect-based fetch leaves initial server output without that fetched result. Hydration does not retroactively change what the server initially sent.','Check where Effects execute.','Has a client commit happened on the server?','react:reference/react/useEffect#caveats')
q('C','Refs','Callback ref cleanup','How can a React 19 callback ref clean up an external registration associated with a DOM node?','Register the node when the callback receives it. Return a cleanup function that removes that registration. Keep setup and cleanup symmetric, including development checks.','Returning cleanup gives the ref its own lifecycle. An implicit return of an unrelated value is not a cleanup contract.','Refs can own imperative resources.','What reverses the registration?','react:reference/react-dom/components/common#ref-callback')
q('C','Suspense','Suspense data sources','Does putting an Effect-based fetch inside Suspense automatically show the boundary’s fallback?','No, arbitrary fetching in Effects does not activate Suspense. The rendered subtree must use a Suspense-enabled source such as lazy or a supported promise-reading integration. Use framework-supported data loading rather than inventing unsupported suspension contracts.','Suspense observes rendering that suspends. A loading boolean changed after an Effect is a separate UI mechanism.','Suspension must be visible during render.','Does the Effect throw or read a supported pending resource during render?','react:reference/react/Suspense#usage')
q('S','Suspense','Boundary granularity','A page has a slow recommendations panel and a fast article. How should Suspense boundaries be arranged for progressive reveal?','Keep the article outside the slow panel’s boundary. Wrap recommendations with a meaningful local fallback. Add nested boundaries only where separate reveal stages improve the experience.','One outer boundary can delay all children together. Excessive tiny boundaries can create distracting loading fragmentation.','Group content that should appear together.','Which section should not wait for recommendations?','react:reference/react/Suspense#revealing-nested-content-as-it-loads')
q('M','Transitions','Urgent input updates','Which update should remain urgent when a text input also triggers an expensive result view?','The state controlling the input’s current text.','Transition updates cannot control a text input’s immediate value reliably. Keep typing responsive and transition or defer the expensive dependent work.','Typing has immediate feedback requirements.','Which value directly controls the input?','react:reference/react/useTransition#updating-an-input-in-a-transition-doesnt-work',wrong=['Every update, including the input value, must be transitioned.','Only analytics counters must remain urgent.','No update can be urgent once useTransition is used.'])
q('C','Transitions','Interruptible rendering','What does marking a state update as a Transition change?','It marks the resulting rendering work as non-urgent. React can interrupt that work for more urgent updates. It does not make synchronous JavaScript inside the callback run on another thread.','Wrapping an expensive synchronous calculation in startTransition does not make the calculation itself nonblocking. The scheduling benefit concerns React rendering updates.','Separate JavaScript execution from render scheduling.','Is there a worker involved?','react:reference/react/useTransition')
q('B','Transitions','Post-await transition updates','Under the documented startTransition async caveat, how should the state update after await remain marked as a Transition?','Wrap the post-await setter in another startTransition: startTransition(() => setResult(data));','The initial transition context does not automatically mark this post-await setter under the documented limitation.~Await resumes later.~Explicitly marking the update preserves its scheduling intent. Handle request ordering separately.','An await creates an asynchronous boundary.','Which setter needs its own marking?','react:reference/react/useTransition#react-doesnt-treat-my-state-update-after-await-as-a-transition','startTransition(async () => {\n  const data = await load();\n  setResult(data);\n});')
q('S','Deferred values','useDeferredValue versus debounce','How does useDeferredValue differ from debouncing search requests?','It lets a dependent UI lag behind a newer value during rendering. It does not impose a fixed delay. It does not by itself prevent a network request for every keystroke.','Deferring render priority and reducing request frequency solve different problems. Use a request policy separately when request volume matters.','Scheduling is not a timer policy.','What actually starts the network request?','react:reference/react/useDeferredValue#how-is-deferring-a-value-different-from-debouncing-and-throttling')
q('W','Deferred values','Deferred result view','Write Search({ items }) using urgent query state and a deferred query for a memoized result list. Items have stable id and name fields.','import { memo, useDeferredValue, useState } from "react";\n\nconst Results = memo(function Results({ items, query }) {\n  return <ul>{items.filter(item => item.name.includes(query))\n    .map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;\n});\n\nexport default function Search({ items }) {\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");\n  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input aria-label="Search" value={query}\n        onChange={event => setQuery(event.target.value)} />\n      <div style={{ opacity: query !== deferredQuery ? 0.6 : 1 }}>\n        <Results items={items} query={deferredQuery} />\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}','Typing updates query urgently.~Results can keep the previous deferred query while background rendering proceeds.~Memoization helps skip the unchanged result props; items identity should remain stable. Filtering remains O(n).','Keep two render priorities for the same input.','Which query should the result list receive?','react:reference/react/useDeferredValue#deferring-re-rendering-for-a-part-of-the-ui','// Implement Search({ items }).\n// items is a stable array supplied by the parent.')
q('C','Optimistic UI','useOptimistic base state','What is the relationship between useOptimistic’s base value and its optimistic updates?','The base value is the authoritative state supplied to the Hook. Optimistic updates temporarily derive a pending view from it. When the Action completes, reconcile with the authoritative result rather than treating the optimistic view as confirmed storage.','Optimism changes perceived latency, not server truth. The update function must remain pure so React can recompute the optimistic view when the base changes.','Separate pending intent from confirmed state.','Which value comes from the successful mutation?','react:reference/react/useOptimistic')
q('B','Optimistic UI','Optimistic mutation','What is unsafe about this useOptimistic reducer, and what expression should replace its body?','It mutates the supplied state. Return a new array: return [...state, message];','push edits the base array in place.~Reusing that array corrupts the distinction between authoritative and optimistic state.~A pure return value can be recalculated safely.','Optimistic calculations are still pure.','Can the original array remain untouched?','react:reference/react/useOptimistic#parameters','const [optimistic, addOptimistic] = useOptimistic(messages,\n  (state, message) => {\n    state.push(message);\n    return state;\n  }\n);')
q('M','Actions','useActionState return','Which tuple does useActionState return?','[state, dispatchAction, isPending]','The Hook manages the latest action result, an action dispatcher and pending state. It does not return an Effect cleanup function or a mutable ref.','Think state, action, progress.','Which member submits the next action?','react:reference/react/useActionState',wrong=['[dispatchAction, cleanup, ref]','[state, setState, dependencyArray]','[promise, cancel, errorBoundary]'])
q('B','Actions','Previous state argument','Why is formData.get failing after wiring save to useActionState?','The action now receives previousState first. Use: async function save(previousState, formData) { ... }','useActionState prepends the current state to the action arguments.~The single parameter therefore receives state, not FormData.~Read the form payload from the second parameter; return the next state.','Check the wrapped action signature.','Which argument does the Hook add?','react:reference/react/useActionState#my-action-can-no-longer-read-the-submitted-form-data','async function save(formData) {\n  return { name: formData.get("name") };\n}\nconst [state, action] = useActionState(save, { name: "" });')
q('W','Actions','Local form Action','Write a complete NameForm using useActionState. Its action trims a submitted name and returns it locally; no network is needed.','import { useActionState } from "react";\n\nasync function save(previousState, formData) {\n  return { name: String(formData.get("name") ?? "").trim() };\n}\n\nexport default function NameForm() {\n  const [state, action, pending] = useActionState(save, { name: "" });\n  return (\n    <form action={action}>\n      <label>Name <input name="name" /></label>\n      <button disabled={pending}>Save</button>\n      <output>{state.name}</output>\n    </form>\n  );\n}','The form supplies FormData to the action dispatcher.~save receives previous state first and returns the new state.~The Hook exposes the result and pending status. Client validation is not server authorization.','Return the next state from the action.','Use the dispatcher as the form action.','react:reference/react/useActionState#using-information-returned-by-a-form-action','// Implement NameForm.\n// This exercise uses a local async action only.')
q('C','Actions','useFormStatus ancestry','Why does useFormStatus usually belong in a SubmitButton rendered inside the form?','It reads the status of a parent form. It does not observe a form returned later by the same component. Put the consumer below the form whose submission it should track.','Hook lookup follows the rendered ancestry. A component cannot read a descendant form’s status merely because it creates that JSX.','The form must be an ancestor.','Where is the Hook relative to the form?','react:reference/react-dom/hooks/useFormStatus')
q('M','use API','Conditional use calls','Which statement about React’s use API is correct?','use can be called in conditions and loops, but still from a component or Hook.','use is an exception to ordinary Hook ordering restrictions. It must not be wrapped in try/catch for promise suspension; use Suspense and error boundaries.','This API has special call rules.','Which restrictions remain despite conditional calls?','react:reference/react/use#caveats',wrong=['Every Hook can now be called conditionally.','use is valid in arbitrary event handlers.','A try/catch around use is the recommended loading boundary.'])
q('S','use API','Stable promise source','A client component creates a new fetch promise during every render and passes it to use. What should change?','Provide a stable promise from a supported cache or framework integration. A Server Component can also create and pass a promise to a Client Component. Recreating uncached promises during client rendering can repeatedly suspend.','A promise is a resource identity, not just a value container. use does not automatically turn arbitrary fresh requests into a deduplicated data cache.','Track promise identity across retries.','Can the same pending resource be reused?','react:reference/react/use#streaming-data-from-the-server-to-the-client')
q('C','use API','Rejected promises','How does use handle a rejected promise during rendering?','It propagates the rejection to the nearest error boundary. Pending promises activate Suspense. To provide an alternate value, transform the promise with catch before passing it to use.','A Suspense fallback represents waiting rather than a complete error recovery policy. Wrapping use in try/catch is not the supported boundary mechanism.','Pending and rejected are different states.','Which boundary handles a rendering failure?','react:reference/react/use#dealing-with-rejected-promises')
q('S','Transitions','Stale async response','Does placing two overlapping search requests in startTransition ensure the newest request’s result wins?','No, request completion can occur out of order. Use an ordering strategy or a framework Action abstraction with the required guarantees. Cancellation or request IDs can prevent obsolete results from committing.','Scheduling priority does not establish network order. A slow older request can still resolve after a newer request without explicit coordination.','Priority and causality differ.','Which response is still relevant?','react:reference/react/useTransition#my-state-updates-in-transitions-are-out-of-order')
q('C','Compiler','React Compiler basics','What does React Compiler automate, and what coding rules still matter?','It can automatically memoize eligible component computations and values. Components and Hooks must still follow React’s purity and Hook rules. Use profiling and compiler diagnostics rather than adding manual memoization everywhere.','Compilation is a performance tool, not permission to mutate render inputs. It also does not remove network latency or replace application architecture.','Automation depends on analyzable semantics.','Which React rules let work be reused safely?','react:learn/react-compiler/introduction')
q('S','Compiler','Manual memo migration','A team enables React Compiler. Should it immediately remove every existing useMemo and useCallback?','Avoid a blanket cleanup without evidence. Verify compiler coverage and behavior first. Remove unnecessary manual caching deliberately, with performance checks where it mattered.','Existing manual memoization can document assumptions or interact with uncompiled code. Compiler adoption is not proof that every cache is irrelevant.','Adoption coverage may be partial.','Which components are actually compiled?','react:learn/react-compiler/introduction#should-i-add-usememo-usecallback-and-reactmemo-everywhere')
q('A','Context','High-frequency updates','Design context usage for stable account identity and rapidly changing cursor coordinates. What split avoids unnecessary account-consumer updates?','Use separate contexts or a suitable store for independently changing data. Keep account consumers subscribed only to account data. Place high-frequency cursor updates close to the UI that needs them.','One combined provider value couples consumers to unrelated updates. memo cannot shield a component from context it actually consumes.','Separate update frequencies.','Which consumers need cursor changes?','react:reference/react/useContext#optimizing-re-renders-when-passing-objects-and-functions')
q('C','Server rendering','Hydration contract','What must the client’s initial render match when hydrating server HTML?','It must describe the same initial content as the server output. Differences should be treated as bugs. Browser-only changes can be applied after hydration through a deliberate client lifecycle.','Hydration attaches React behavior to existing markup. It is not a guaranteed general-purpose patcher for arbitrary server/client disagreement.','Compare initial renders.','Did time, randomness or locale change the markup?','react:reference/react-dom/client/hydrateRoot#caveats')
q('B','Hydration','Random initial markup','Why can this ID cause hydration problems, and what should replace its generation?','Math.random differs between server and client. Use a top-level useId() call for this label/input relationship.','The server generates one random ID.~The client’s first render generates another.~A coordinated ID avoids mismatched attributes; data keys should still come from data rather than useId.','The first two renders must agree.','Which React API coordinates relationship IDs?','react:reference/react/useId','function Field() {\n  const id = String(Math.random());\n  return <label htmlFor={id}>Name<input id={id} /></label>;\n}')
q('S','Effects','Effect Event use case','An Effect connects by roomId but should log with the latest theme without reconnecting on theme changes. What does useEffectEvent offer?','Move the nonreactive logging behavior into an Effect Event. It can read the latest values when called from the Effect. Keep roomId as a real Effect dependency and do not use this API to hide required dependencies.','Effect Events separate nonreactive logic from synchronization triggers. They are not general callbacks to pass around or invoke from arbitrary event handlers.','Separate the reconnect trigger from latest-value reads.','Which value should change the subscription?','react:reference/react/useEffectEvent')
q('C','Activity','Hidden subtree lifecycle','What is the purpose of React’s Activity boundary when its mode becomes hidden?','It can preserve a subtree’s state while hiding its UI. Its Effects are cleaned up while hidden. When visible again, Effects are recreated with the preserved state.','Keeping state is different from keeping every external subscription active. Activity is not simply an unconditional guarantee that all hidden work continues normally.','State and Effects have different lifecycles.','Which resources should pause while hidden?','react:reference/react/Activity')
q('M','Suspense','Already visible content','Which mechanism can help avoid replacing already revealed content with a fallback during a non-urgent update?','Mark the relevant update as a Transition.','Transitions can keep revealed content visible while new content prepares. Newly introduced boundaries and urgent updates still have their own fallback behavior.','Preserve useful content during waiting.','Is the update urgent?','react:reference/react/Suspense#preventing-already-revealed-content-from-hiding',wrong=['Delete all Suspense boundaries.','Mutate React’s internal pending flag.','Use a random key on every render.'])
q('U','Optimistic UI','Optimistic failure recovery','Follow-up to REACT-078: what should happen when the server rejects an optimistic edit?','Reconcile back to confirmed state or present an explicit recoverable failure. Explain the failure near the affected action. Preserve retryable user input when appropriate rather than claiming the edit succeeded.','Optimistic rendering is provisional. A pending view must not become authoritative merely because it was displayed first.','An optimistic result is not confirmation.','What data is still known to be correct?','react:reference/react/useOptimistic')
q('A','State architecture','URL and local state ownership','For a shareable search page, how should URL filters, a temporary open dropdown and server results be owned?','Keep shareable filters in the routing or URL state model. Keep transient dropdown visibility local. Let the data layer own fetched results and derive views from those sources.','Duplicating each value into several independent stores creates synchronization problems. State structure should reflect persistence, sharing and ownership requirements.','Different values have different lifetimes.','Which state must survive a shared link?','react:learn/choosing-the-state-structure')
q('S','Performance','Profiler interpretation','A Profiler reports high render duration for a list. What should be checked before introducing memoization?','Confirm the slow interaction in a representative build. Inspect component work, state placement and changing props. Apply an optimization to the measured bottleneck and compare the result.','Memoization can help repeated unchanged work but not every source of latency. Development diagnostics and profiling overhead can affect measurements.','Measure the actual interaction.','Which computation consumes the time?','react:reference/react/Profiler')
q('U','Refs','Imperative versus declarative API','Follow-up to REACT-052: should a modal expose open() when its visibility can be expressed with an isOpen prop?','Prefer an isOpen prop for declarative visibility. Reserve an imperative handle for operations such as focus that genuinely require it. A clear ownership model prevents parent and child visibility state from drifting.','Imperative handles are an escape hatch. Exposing more methods does not automatically make a component easier to coordinate.','Ask who owns visibility.','Can a prop describe the desired state?','react:reference/react/useImperativeHandle#pitfall')
q('A','Architecture','Async screen boundaries','Design loading, error and optimistic states for a saveable editor without treating them as one boolean.','Track authoritative data and local draft separately. Represent pending submission and optimistic presentation explicitly. Use loading boundaries for suspended reads and error handling appropriate to failed reads or writes.','A single loading flag cannot distinguish initial loading, background refresh and failed mutation. Clear state ownership prevents a failed save from silently discarding a valid draft.','List independent state dimensions.','Can loading and an existing draft coexist?','react:learn/reacting-to-input-with-state')
